## ffmpeg

Role: decoding, stills, and (where a platform encoder is used) muxing.
The release build is an LGPL build produced by `scripts/build-ffmpeg-*.sh`.
Those scripts refuse to finish if the result is not LGPL. The development
machine's Homebrew ffmpeg may be GPL; that binary is not what the app ships.

How it is combined with Video Butler:

- macOS: dynamic libraries inside `video_butler.app/Contents/Frameworks`,
  loaded by the in-process LibAv backend.
- iOS: dynamic frameworks embedded in the app, so the LGPL libraries can be
  replaced.
- Android: `libav*.so` and `libswscale.so` in the APK, loaded by soname next
  to the engine.
- Linux and the Windows engine build: dynamic libraries beside the engine
  (`RUNPATH $ORIGIN` on Linux). The Windows application itself is developed
  in `charlesbuling/VideoBulter-windows` and is not part of this 0.2.0 release.
- The desktop CLI can instead spawn an `ffmpeg` binary (`FfmpegCli`). That
  path is not the mobile app.

The editor's export uses the platform encoder (VideoToolbox on Apple) and
libav's AAC. It does not ship x264, x265, `drawtext`, or `eq`.

Upstream: https://ffmpeg.org/ — LGPL version 2.1 or later for the build
this project produces. ffmpeg's own source notices travel with that build.

## whisper.cpp

Role: speech recognition behind `AsrAdapter`, when the `whisper` feature is
on (macOS, Android, iOS, and a native Linux build).

Upstream: https://github.com/ggml-org/whisper.cpp — MIT.
The ggml weights are conversions of OpenAI Whisper, also MIT. See the model
list below. whisper.cpp is linked into the engine. It is not the same
binary as llama.cpp: each carries its own ggml, so they are not linked
together.

## llama.cpp

Role: vision-language models (captions, and the composition judge when its
files are present). Built into `vb-llama-engine`, a separate dynamic
library with a small C ABI and no exported ggml symbols. `vb-llama` loads
that library at runtime.

Upstream: https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp — MIT.
Not part of the iOS build, and not yet part of the Linux or Windows engine
builds.

## ONNX Runtime

Role: depth, face detection, person masks, body pose, scene labels, and
AuraFace face grouping. Loaded at runtime (`ort` with `load-dynamic`).
Nothing in the engine links it.

Upstream: https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime — MIT.
The macOS copy is built from source for this app's deployment target. The
Android copy is Microsoft's Android build, fetched by
`scripts/fetch-onnxruntime-android.sh`. Not part of the iOS build, and not
yet part of the Linux or Windows engine builds.

